// ALG1 Unit 03 Lesson 08 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Putting It All Together
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.LR.1; MLA.A1.LR.2; MLA.A1.LR.3; MLA.A1.LR.5; MLA.A1.FN.5; MLA.A1.AR.2; MLA.A1.AR.3; MLA.A1.AR.4
::ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.5
Question:
What is the slope between (1,4) and (3,10)?
{
~A. 2#This is the change in x only.
=B. 3#Correct. The slope is (10-4)/(3-1)=6/2=3.
~C. 6#This is the change in y only.
~D. 14#This adds the y-values instead of finding a rate of change.
}

::ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.3
Question:
Which equation has slope 4 and y-intercept -2?
{
~A. y=-2x+4#This reverses the slope and y-intercept.
~B. y=4x+2#The slope is correct, but the y-intercept sign is wrong.
~C. y=-4x-2#The y-intercept is correct, but the slope sign is wrong.
=D. y=4x-2#Correct. In y=mx+b, m=4 and b=-2.
}

::ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.2
Question:
Solve x-5\le 12.
{
=A. x\le17#Correct. Add 5 to both sides.
~B. x\ge17#The inequality direction does not change when adding 5.
~C. x\le7#This subtracts 5 instead of adding 5.
~D. x\ge7#This uses the wrong operation and direction.
}

::ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.3
Question:
For y>2x+1, which boundary line should be graphed?
{
~A. Solid y=2x+1#A strict inequality uses a dashed boundary.
~B. Solid x=2y+1#This changes the equation and uses the wrong boundary style.
=C. Dashed y=2x+1#Correct. The boundary is y=2x+1, and strict > means dashed.
~D. Dashed y=1x+2#This switches the slope and intercept.
}

::ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L08_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2
Question:
A line has slope 2 and passes through (0,5). Which equation represents the line?
{
~A. y=5x+2#This reverses slope and y-intercept.
=B. y=2x+5#Correct. The slope is 2 and the y-intercept is 5.
~C. y=2x-5#The slope is correct, but the y-intercept sign is wrong.
~D. y=x+7#This does not use the given slope or intercept.
}

